.TH BASALT-PROMPT 1 2026-10-04 "Basalt OS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
basalt-prompt \- the Basalt OS shell prompt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B basalt-prompt
.RB { off | on | status | preview | bench
.RI [ N ]
.RB | zsh }
.SH DESCRIPTION
Basalt OS sets a compact, colored prompt for interactive
.BR bash (1)
shells, from
.IR /etc/profile.d/basalt-prompt.sh .
Scripts, non-interactive shells and
.B bash -c
are never changed. The prompt is plain bash, with no other program run
except one time-boxed
.B git status
inside a git work tree.
.PP
From left to right, each part only when it applies:
.TP
.B \[u25C6] agent 0123ab
the shell runs inside a basalt-agent session (its cgroup slice, its SELinux
domain, or BASALT_AGENT_SESSION), white on terra roxa;
.TP
.B \[u2B22] name
the shell runs in a container or toolbox (the name when known);
.TP
.B user@host
over SSH, as root, in a container, and outside a local graphical session
(a server console); root is shown in red with a
.B #
sign;
.TP
.B ~/\[u2026]/dir
the working directory, its last components only when longer than
.IR path_max ;
.TP
.B main+*?\(ua1\(da2
the git branch (or
.B @
and the commit when detached), with
.B |rebase
or
.B |merge
during those, and marks:
.B +
staged,
.B *
modified,
.B ?
untracked (when enabled),
.B \(ua
and
.B \(da
commits ahead of and behind the upstream,
.B ~
not checked (the repository is on a network file system, or git took longer
than the time box once in this shell);
.TP
.B \[u2717]130 INT
the exit status of the last command when it failed, with the signal name;
.TP
.B &2
background jobs.
.PP
The prompt sign follows:
.B \[u276F]
(or
.B >
in ASCII), red after a failure, and
.B #
for root.
.SH COLORS AND SYMBOLS
The colors are the Basalt OS palette. Truecolor when COLORTERM is truecolor or
24bit (or the terminal is foot, kitty, Alacritty, WezTerm, Ghostty), 256
colors when TERM names a 256-color terminal, 16 colors otherwise, none with
TERM=dumb. A non-empty
.B NO_COLOR
turns colors off. Unicode symbols are used on UTF-8 locales; the Linux
console, serial terminals and other locales get ASCII. Neither needs a
special font.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/basalt/prompt.conf
holds the system defaults (all keys listed, commented out), and
.I ~/.config/basalt/prompt.conf
overrides them for one user, one
.B key = value
per line. A
.BI BASALT_PROMPT_ KEY
environment variable overrides both, e.g.
.BR BASALT_PROMPT_LINES=2 .
.TP
.B enable
yes, or no for the distribution's plain prompt.
.B BASALT_PROMPT=off
does the same for one shell.
.TP
.B glyphs
auto, unicode or ascii.
.TP
.B color
auto, truecolor, 256, 16 or none.
.TP
.B theme
auto (tones readable on dark and light backgrounds, or the one COLORFGBG
indicates), dark or light.
.TP
.B lines
1, or 2 for the information on one line and the prompt sign below.
.TP
.B host
auto, always or never.
.TP
.B path_max
40; 0 shows the whole path.
.TP
.BR git ", " git_dirty ", " git_untracked ", " git_timeout_ms
yes, yes, no, 200.
.TP
.BR show_status ", " show_jobs ", " show_agent ", " show_container
yes.
.PP
A PS1 set after the prompt is loaded (in
.IR ~/.bashrc ,
for example) is kept: the prompt stops updating it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B off
Turn the prompt off for the current user (writes
.B enable = no
to
.IR ~/.config/basalt/prompt.conf ).
.B on
turns it back on. Both apply to new shells.
.TP
.B status
The effective settings and what was detected: colors, symbols, SSH,
container, agent session, git.
.TP
.B preview
Print the prompt for the current directory and a few sample states.
.TP
.BI bench " " \fR[\fIN\fR]
Build the prompt N times (200) in the current directory and print the time
per prompt.
.TP
.B zsh
Add the zsh version of the prompt to
.IR ~/.zshrc .
fish uses it by itself (from fish's vendor configuration directory) unless
the user has a prompt of their own.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/profile.d/basalt-prompt.sh
.br
.I /usr/share/basalt-prompt/basalt-prompt.bash
.br
.I /usr/share/basalt-prompt/basalt-prompt.zsh
.br
.I /usr/share/fish/vendor_conf.d/basalt-prompt.fish
.br
.I /etc/basalt/prompt.conf
.br
.I ~/.config/basalt/prompt.conf
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1)
